# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Recorded and derived data. This is the important one.
#
# data/live/books.jsonl alone is ~43 GB, data/uniform ~13 GB and
# data/viewer_full ~13 GB. GitHub rejects any single file over 100 MB and
# warns past a ~5 GB repo, so none of this can be committed. All of it is
# reproducible from the recorder output; only the small MLB Stats API window
# file is worth keeping in the repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/*
!data/game_windows.json

# Analysis OUTPUTS are small and worth versioning -- they are the findings,
# not bulk data. 4 MB total: the per-event CSV, the figures, and the cached
# aggregate arrays (which stand in for ~25 minutes of recomputation).
!data/collapse/
!data/collapse/**

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keeping small artifacts out of an excluded directory takes three steps, not
# one. `data/*` above only matches one level deep, so un-ignoring a directory
# makes git descend into it and NOTHING inside is excluded any more. A first
# attempt at this used just `!data/jump/` and `!data/jump/*.csv`, which
# un-ignored the directory and thereby staged lob_books_2026-08-30.npy
# (1.4 GB) and feat_books_hkt0909.parquet (116 MB) -- both over GitHub's
# 100 MB hard limit, so the push would have failed after uploading gigabytes.
#
# The correct shape is: un-ignore the directory, RE-ignore its contents, then
# un-ignore only the specific files. Verify any change with
#     git check-ignore -v <path>
# rather than assuming, because the failure mode is silent until push.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Jump-study result tables, run configs and logs. Small, and they are the
# findings. feat_*.parquet and lob_*.npy stay out -- both rebuild from the
# recorder output via jump_data.py.
!data/jump/
data/jump/*
!data/jump/*.csv
!data/jump/*.json
!data/jump/*.txt

# Trained model bundles: weights (2.4 MB across five models) plus the
# manifests that make them reloadable -- architecture args, data spec,
# preprocessing constants, decision threshold, metrics, source hashes. These
# ARE the artifacts; a bare state_dict with no manifest is unusable, which is
# how the first CNN-Transformer result was lost. The .npy prediction dumps
# beside them (9.7 MB) stay out; regenerate with
# `python model_registry.py --verify`.
!data/models/
data/models/*
!data/models/*/
data/models/*/*
!data/models/*/weights.pt
!data/models/*/manifest.json

# Generated viewer page (rebuild with build_viewer_multi.py; it embeds the
# manifest from data/viewer_full/index.json, so it churns on every rebuild).
viewer_full.html

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
env/
*.egg-info/

# Logs and scratch
*.log

# Unattended run logs
logs/

# Research caches: generated datasets, rebuilt by build_dataset.py.
# The prediction-point parquets are ~1 GB; the .npy prediction dumps beside
# the metrics are regenerable from train.py.
research/*/cache/
results/jump_prediction/*.npy
